WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.600 --> 00:00:06.160
It might sound like a random pairing,

00:00:06.360 --> 00:00:10.880
like Marie Curie on Wikileaks,
or Charles Dickens on twerking.

00:00:11.320 --> 00:00:12.320
But it isn't.

00:00:13.160 --> 00:00:16.680
We know her as a nurse,
the lady with the lamp,

00:00:16.760 --> 00:00:19.520
gliding through
blood-soaked army hospitals.

00:00:19.920 --> 00:00:23.200
But maybe we should call her
the lady with the bar chart,

00:00:23.280 --> 00:00:25.240
because she was
at least as illuminating

00:00:25.320 --> 00:00:27.040
in the world of statistics.

00:00:27.480 --> 00:00:30.760
So, what would she think of big data?

00:00:31.200 --> 00:00:33.400
Florence loved statistics.

00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:37.160
She said she found them
"more enlivening than a novel".

00:00:37.880 --> 00:00:41.840
And a friend said,
"However exhausted Florence might be,

00:00:41.920 --> 00:00:46.280
the sight of long columns of figures
was perfectly reviving to her."

00:00:46.840 --> 00:00:49.800
For Florence,
statistics were God's work.

00:00:50.400 --> 00:00:52.920
She said,
"To understand God's thoughts

00:00:53.000 --> 00:00:57.360
we must study statistics, for these
are the measure of his purpose."

00:00:58.680 --> 00:01:02.160
So, although she's
famous now as a nurse,

00:01:02.240 --> 00:01:05.520
she actually trained first
as a statistician.

00:01:05.960 --> 00:01:08.800
It wasn't until
the beginning of the 1840s -

00:01:09.080 --> 00:01:10.840
when she was in her early twenties

00:01:10.920 --> 00:01:13.600
and saw hunger and unemployment
all around her -

00:01:13.920 --> 00:01:15.680
that she became a nurse.

00:01:16.840 --> 00:01:19.080
She'd combined
her nursing and statistics

00:01:19.160 --> 00:01:21.640
to become an experienced
hospital manager,

00:01:21.720 --> 00:01:23.800
just in time for the Crimean War.

00:01:24.200 --> 00:01:28.360
At the time, nurses were seen
as ignorant and lower class.

00:01:28.840 --> 00:01:30.600
But Florence changed that.

00:01:30.920 --> 00:01:34.640
Florence volunteered to lead
a team of nurses in the war.

00:01:35.200 --> 00:01:38.680
Before she arrived, the military
hospitals didn't even bother

00:01:38.760 --> 00:01:40.680
to record many of the deaths.

00:01:41.400 --> 00:01:43.720
Florence collected data
about everything,

00:01:44.040 --> 00:01:47.640
so she could show that changes
in diet and sanitation

00:01:47.920 --> 00:01:53.440
had brought the hospital's death rate
down from 42% to just 2%.

00:01:55.400 --> 00:01:59.200
It's only when you gather data
methodically that patterns emerge.

00:01:59.720 --> 00:02:02.520
Things that were hidden
suddenly become clear.

00:02:03.400 --> 00:02:06.760
That might sound obvious now,
but it wasn't back then.

00:02:07.560 --> 00:02:10.160
Florence's work made a huge impact

00:02:10.240 --> 00:02:13.240
and laid the groundwork
for things we now take for granted -

00:02:13.600 --> 00:02:16.160
like being able to compare
hospitals' performance,

00:02:16.240 --> 00:02:18.320
or just the fact that
hospitals are clean.

00:02:19.320 --> 00:02:22.800
Florence showed what could be
achieved by following the evidence,

00:02:23.080 --> 00:02:26.600
instead of gut instinct,
prejudice or tradition.

00:02:27.480 --> 00:02:32.720
Another little-known side of Florence
is her talent for infographics.

00:02:33.080 --> 00:02:35.600
She turned data into pictures,

00:02:35.680 --> 00:02:40.080
making it impossible for MPs
and civil servants to ignore.

00:02:40.600 --> 00:02:44.480
So, she would love the
data journalism we have today.

00:02:44.800 --> 00:02:48.320
She would love the way big data
makes all this possible.

00:02:49.000 --> 00:02:52.760
But she'd hate some of the ways
that data are abused.

00:02:53.720 --> 00:02:56.160
She knew that people
can game the system

00:02:56.240 --> 00:02:58.840
to make their performance
indicators look better.

00:02:59.680 --> 00:03:03.800
So, when you hear of hospitals
fiddling operation waiting times,

00:03:04.040 --> 00:03:05.600
think of Florence.

00:03:06.120 --> 00:03:10.080
And I think she'd be appalled
at using data to target adverts

00:03:10.280 --> 00:03:12.640
and manipulate people
on social media.

00:03:13.120 --> 00:03:15.240
This would not be God's work.

00:03:16.000 --> 00:03:19.840
Although largely confined to her room
for over half a century,

00:03:20.200 --> 00:03:24.520
she worked tirelessly behind the
scenes in coordinating campaigns,

00:03:24.720 --> 00:03:27.600
and she always had
a careful media strategy.

00:03:28.200 --> 00:03:31.880
So, I think she might like other
aspects of social media.

00:03:32.080 --> 00:03:35.720
She'd enjoy the opportunity
to communicate on a grand scale,

00:03:36.000 --> 00:03:37.560
with ideas going viral

00:03:37.640 --> 00:03:41.000
and so many people being able
to take part in the debate.

00:03:41.600 --> 00:03:43.720
Her compassion brought her fame.

00:03:44.040 --> 00:03:48.640
And she used that fame ruthlessly,
along with her incredible intellect,

00:03:48.720 --> 00:03:51.800
to save lives
on an unprecedented scale.

00:03:52.560 --> 00:03:56.080
If she were alive now,
she'd challenge us to do the same.

00:03:56.680 --> 00:04:00.240
To look at how we can use
the vast amount of data now available

00:04:00.320 --> 00:04:01.640
to save lives.

00:04:02.200 --> 00:04:04.160
To make the world a better place.

00:04:04.760 --> 00:04:07.360
To shine a little more light
on us all.

